#. 完整操作说明 | 运行
msgid "TXT_0462"
msgstr ""
"[GOAL AND NUMBERS]\n"
"Reveal every safe cell and correctly flag every pollution core. A number counts the pollution cores adjacent to that cell. Flags are your deductions; an incorrect flag is not treated as a correct answer. When a revealed number is satisfied and every surrounding flag is correct, double-click it to reveal the remaining adjacent cells.\n\n"
"[MOUSE CONTROLS]\n"
"Land/Ocean: left-click to reveal, right-click to flag, and double-click a revealed number to chord. Sky: left-click to reveal, right-click to flag, drag to rotate the sphere, and double-click a number to chord. When Eco Scan is charged, press C or click the robot, then choose a hidden cell; right-click or K cancels targeting. Esc opens or closes the pause menu.\n\n"
"[KEYBOARD CONTROLS]\n"
"After selecting Keyboard mode in Settings: use the arrow keys or W S A D to move the cell cursor, J to reveal or confirm a number, and K to flag. In Sky, the arrow keys select an adjacent face, W S A D rotates the sphere, J reveals, K flags, and R centers the selected face. C activates Eco Scan. In menus, use the arrow keys and Enter/Space. F11 or Alt+Enter changes display mode.\n\n"
"[ECO SCAN]\n"
"Reveals and the first flag placed on a cell build energy, which carries between levels. Three lights above the robot show the three charge stages. At full charge, a “Press C or click the robot” prompt appears. Scan identifies one hidden cell as safe or polluted, then must recharge.\n\n"
"[LEVELS 1–7: LAND, UP TO 8 SQUARE NEIGHBORS]\n"
"Level 1: 5×5, 5 cores, eight-direction adjacency, with the first-action tutorial.\n"
"Level 2: 6×6, 8 cores, eight-direction adjacency.\n"
"Level 3: 8×8, 9 cores. Ruins cannot be revealed, flagged, or scanned; ignore them when reading numbers.\n"
"Level 4: 9×9, 12 cores. Random obstacle clusters reshape the playable area.\n"
"Level 5: 10×10, 13 cores, 2 pollution nodes. A node counts as pollution and cleanses after all surrounding cells are handled correctly.\n"
"Level 6: 12×12, 17 cores, 3 pollution nodes, using the same rule across more node regions.\n"
"Level 7: 13×13, 20 cores, 3 nodes and the mother tree. Resolve the three root anchors to cleanse the boss.\n\n"
"[LEVELS 8–14: OCEAN, UP TO 6 HEX NEIGHBORS]\n"
"Only hexes sharing an edge are adjacent. Coral walls cut shared edges; numbers, zero expansion, and chording never cross a wall.\n"
"Level 8: 6×6, 7 cores, basic six-direction adjacency.\n"
"Level 9: 8×7, 10 cores, one coral wall changes adjacency.\n"
"Level 10: 9×8, 14 cores, two coral walls.\n"
"Level 11: 10×9, 17 cores. Two debris groups drift right on a timer; each contains two safe buoys and two mines. Adjacency and numbers recalculate after every move.\n"
"Level 12: 11×10, 21 cores. Three faster debris groups keep changing adjacency in real time.\n"
"Level 13: 12×11, 25 cores. A coral wall and two tidal zones limit play to the lit zone; completing it unlocks the next and updates boundary numbers.\n"
"Level 14: 13×12, 30 cores. Boss level with two coral walls and three tidal zones completed in order.\n\n"
"[LEVELS 15–21: SKY, SHARED-EDGE SPHERE ADJACENCY]\n"
"Pentagons have 5 neighbors and hexagons have 6. Only faces sharing an edge on the sphere are adjacent; rear faces remain part of the same board.\n"
"Level 15: 32 faces, 6 cores, basic fullerene.\n"
"Level 16: 42 faces, 8 cores, expanded sphere.\n"
"Level 17: 92 faces, 15 cores. Revealing a safe face has a 20% chance to trigger Breeze Aid, revealing a random safe face or auto-flagging a core.\n"
"Level 18: 162 faces, 26 cores, retaining the 20% Breeze Aid.\n"
"Level 19: 252 faces, 40 cores, Breeze Aid plus one Cloud Shield with a 70% trigger chance.\n"
"Level 20: 362 faces, 58 cores, Breeze Aid, one Cloud Shield, and three Storm Eye pollution cores. The Eye itself cannot be operated; revealing every safe neighbor and correctly flagging nearby pollution breaks it automatically. Defeat all three to win.\n"
"Level 21: 492 faces, 78 cores, Breeze Aid, Cloud Shield, and five Storm Eye pollution cores. Surround and break all five as exploration advances to win.\n\n"
"[UNIVERSE EXPEDITION]\n"
"Star nodes are adjacent only when joined by a glowing line; screen distance does not matter. Straight lines, curves, and purple wormholes all count. A number counts pollution on every directly connected node. The ship has two shields: touching pollution consumes a shield and auto-flags that node first. Graph grammars keep generating new boards. Every genuinely new Universe board adds 1 to the lifetime exploration count; failure, leaving, or starting a new expedition never resets it.\n\n"
"[BADGES AND BADGE BOOK]\n"
"After a Land victory, that level's badge spins and grows at the center of the screen amid a golden glow. Click any mouse button, or press Space, Enter, or any gamepad button to store it in the Badge Book. When opened from victory, left-click to enter the next level and right-click to return to the current victory screen; the bottom-right button opens the Badge Hall. Badge buttons on the welcome screen, map, and Settings open the Badge Hall, where the Land, Ocean, and Sky covers open their matching Badge Books. Click any blank area outside the four ecology showcases, or press Esc, to return. Completed-level ecology and badges light automatically. Universe has no Badge Book: its showcase records lifetime exploration with ones-place asteroids, tens-place large planets, and hundreds-place star clusters. At 1,000, a rotating galaxy and the ultimate trophy remain permanently on display. Chinese and English each use badge artwork with matching embedded text.\n\n"
"[SETTINGS AND ASSISTS]\n"
"Settings separately control master volume, BGM, SFX, display mode, mouse/keyboard operation mode, and language. First-click protection guarantees a safe opening; repeated Land failures strengthen adaptive opening assistance. The pause menu can resume, restart, return to the map, open Settings, return to the main menu, or quit. This guide is available from the map, Settings, and the in-game pause menu."

